// Swarm — the moving-objects showpiece. Sixteen sprites bounce round the
// picture, on a frame timeline that shows the title, releases them, opens
// the border so they fly through it (on a machine whose border opens),
// closes it again, and rotates their colours — one program, nine targets:
//
//   - C64: hardware sprites, sixteen from the VIC's eight, reused down
//     the frame by @8bitscript/sprites' C64 twin over
//     @8bitscript/c64/multiplex, through the top and bottom border when
//     the scene opens it.
//   - PET: eight quadrant-block rings at 4-pixel steps, the sprites
//     package's PET twin, restoring what they cover — the 32K 3032, whose
//     4K sibling the 2001 has no room for the twin's tables.
//   - the other seven: sixteen glyphs on the character grid, the sprites
//     package's portable cell layer — no border to open, so Open and
//     Close do nothing and the sprites stay in the playfield. Honest, and
//     free: sprites.MAX and sprites.EXTENDS are consts, and what a machine
//     cannot do folds away.
//
// ---- where the C64's frame goes ------------------------------------------
//
// Measured with CIA 2's timer A around the loop, 2026-09-19, sixteen
// sprites with eight reused, recorded per frame and printed once: the
// whole iteration is ~15,300 cycles of the NTSC frame's 17,095 — the
// scene ~400, the flock's motion ~3,500, sprites.update() ~11,000 — and
// the C64 runs the loop at one hardware frame an iteration, losing a
// frame on about half the frames that also recolour all sixteen (FRAME
// 776 at `--frames 1000`, where 789 is every frame). Those numbers carry
// what the VIC takes out of every window they were read in: bad lines,
// the sprites' own DMA, and the raster handler's ten-odd interrupts a
// frame — about 40% of the update's window is that, not the routine.
//
// It was two frames an iteration the same morning: multiplex.update()
// alone was ~16,000 cycles in the compiler's generic 6502 code, three
// to four instructions where hand assembly has one (the same finding as
// @8bitscript/c64/text's print). The update is native now
// (packages/c64/native/6502/multiplex.s, ~9,500 with the steal inside),
// and two habits of this program's own paid for the rest, each measured:
// the counter is five digits kept in place and one putChar a frame
// (counter.8bs — a five-digit printNumber every frame was ~2,800), and
// the colours are set from one place so the call inlines (flock.8bs's
// recolor). Nothing here hides the cost: FRAME counts iterations, and a
// screenshot at `--frames N` shows about N - 211 of them.
//
// The frame loop is here; *what happens when* is Scene.8bx (the cues,
// composed), *how the sprites move* is flock.8bs (the arithmetic), and
// *what a sprite looks like* is shapes.8bs with a C64 and a PET twin
// beside it, and *the count on screen* is counter.8bs.
// docs/project/frame.md is the design; measured sizes are in ../README.md.
//
// ---- reading a screenshot ------------------------------------------------
//
//   - Before frame 60: the title and the counter only.
//   - From 60: the sprites, bouncing. On the C64 rings in four colours;
//     on the PET eight block rings; on a cell machine letter O's — both
//     below the two text rows.
//   - From 180 to 420 on the C64: rings above line 50 and below 250 —
//     in the border, where nothing draws on a C64 that has not opened it.
//   - Every 32 frames the colours step along white, yellow, cyan, green.
//   - FRAME climbs by one a frame; a frozen picture and an idle one differ.
import { screen } from "@8bitscript/screen";
import { sprites } from "@8bitscript/sprites";
import { timeline } from "@8bitscript/timeline";
import { flock } from "./flock.8bs";
import { Scene } from "./Scene.8bx";

export function main(): void {
    screen.blank();
    flock.begin();
    timeline.start();
    while (true) {
        waitFrame();
        timeline.tick();
        Scene();
        flock.move();
        sprites.update();
    }
}
